+# Bounded work-item discovery
+
+The existing authenticated `GET /project/:projectID/accessibility/workqueue`
+accepts `view=bounded-v2` for its **items** list. The mounted normal work queue
+uses this profile. Remedy remains default-off and tenant/project/session policy
+is unchanged. Historical recovery-only page/status reads retain their existing
+legacy list path, independently of this items profile.
+
+## Request and response
+
+- `limit` defaults to 100; only decimal integers 1–100 are accepted. Invalid values,
+  repeated/structured parameters, unknown views/statuses and malformed cursors
+  return 400. Omit `view` for the unchanged legacy default 100/max 200 contract.
+- `status` defaults to `open`. Values are `all`, `open`, `todo`, `in_progress`,
+  `needs_review`, `verified`, `blocked`. `open` means the four non-verified
+  statuses. The server applies it **before** both count and batch selection.
+- Items remain ordered by `_id DESC`, excluding history and scan-evidence arrays.
+  Existing `items` and `nextCursor` fields remain. `lists.items` adds `total`
+  (the full matching population at this request's upper-ID fence),`returned`,
+  `hasMore`,`complete`, and an explicit `countConsistency` statement.
+- `complete:true` means this one response contains the whole matching list:
+  first request, no later batch, and returned equals total. A final continuation
+  has `hasMore:false` but `complete:false`; it is not the whole list. No accumulated
+  all-history representation is introduced in the browser.
+- `sectionID` still selects the page whose **candidate findings** are shown.
+  It does not filter queue items. Page/candidate/history lists do not gain v2
+  completeness or snapshot semantics in this change.
+
+Use the returned opaque `nextCursor` with the same project/view/status. It binds
+kind/profile/project/filter, the initial largest item ID and the last returned ID.
+Legacy and v2 cursors are not interchangeable. The upper-ID fence excludes newer
+IDs; it is not a transaction, timestamp snapshot or frozen status population.
+Statuses can change between requests; count and batch are separate observed
+queries. Backfilled older IDs and status edits may change membership. Refresh
+starts a new traversal; it is required to see edits in an already-passed range.
+The UI resets continuations on project/filter changes and successful edits and
+keeps only the displayed batch. Old delayed responses cannot populate a new
+project or overwrite a newer filter result.
+
+The complete v2 JSON response is limited to 1 MiB. Oversized evidence/metadata
+returns 413 with a smaller-limit/full-export instruction; records, notes and
+URLs are never silently truncated. The legacy response remains compatible.
+`GET .../items/report` remains a separate authenticated complete streaming export,
+with all retained history/scan evidence, its existing deadline/backpressure/
+cancellation behavior, and terminal `complete:true` only on successful completion.
+A bounded list is not a backup or replacement for that export.
+
+## Indexes and operations
+
+Enabled Remedy storage initializes two additional non-unique indexes:
+`{projectID:1,_id:-1}` and `{projectID:1,status:1,_id:-1}`. Real Mongo 7 execution
+plans on the 500-item/125k-event fixture showed index-only creation-fence counts
+(0 document fetches instead of 500) and100 instead of 500 documents examined for
+the verified batch. Open-batch plans still examined 201 documents; no hint is
+forced and no production/multi-tenant latency claim is made. The indexed fields
+already exist; there is no data backfill or new collection. Disabled Remedy does
+not initialize optional storage. Existing collection/index privileges and useful
+storage-unavailable handling still apply.
+
+Operators must account for index build time/storage and include index definitions
+in reviewed backup/restore/upgrade rehearsal. These are synthetic standalone
+Mongo 7 observations, not validation of an unknown production Atlas tier or host.
+No index build/drop or migration on a live deployment is authorized here. An
+application rollback can retain these non-unique indexes; do not delete journals
+or routine-drop indexes to roll back a client. Earlier clients keep their legacy
+list contract. Preserve the coordinated storage/recovery guides when rolling
+back a full Remedy delivery.
+
+Queue status edits still append their attributed versioned note. They do not fix
+book content, change WCAG criterion reviews or establish conformance. Full
+scan/preview/signed apply/restore/recovery and optional review-only mode remain
+one implementation. LibreTexts integration/deployment owns vendor conditional-
+write validation, write enablement and rollout timing. This list change grants
+no production deployment/write/provider/SSO/release-owner acceptance.
